WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.120 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  kept you waiting on Metal Gear Solid's  hallmarks have never been represented  better than in Metal Gear Solid 5 Ground  zeroes it combines tense action and  best-in-class cutscene direction that  stands above any other game in the  series but naturally due to it being a  prologue the narrative ultimately  concludes harsh sooner than you'd like  but on the other hand after the credits  roll and a handful of side missions  unlock the best parts of ground zeroes  are just getting started the story  sequences here captivate with impressive  cinematography and the Metal Gear has  always been recognized for having  high-quality cutscenes they're usually  spotted by inconsistent animation and  over-the-top voice acting thankfully the  opposite is true here characters move  and speak with such natural grace and  even though it's jarring to hear the  recognizable Kiefer Sutherland voice big  boss and place a fan favorite David  Hayter his delivery is far more  realistic and believable at the end of  the day your mission begins in a typical  dramatic fashion on a dark and stormy  night at Camp Omega a military black  site on the coast of Cuba you need to  infiltrate the heavily guarded base to  rescue a member of your entourage and  with only a shred of evidence to point  you in the right direction you'll have  to rely on your powers of observation  eavesdropping in some light  interrogation to succeed as such ground  zeroes is designed for stealth gameplay  and with a minimal HUD a narrow  third-person camera angle and a  sprawling base filled with threats  sneaking behind enemy lines has rarely  felt this tense unlike older games in  the series there's no automated radar to  keep tabs on nearby enemies the only way  of tracking an enemy is to first spot  them with your own eyes and only then  after marking them with your binoculars  can you follow their movements should  someone catch you in the act you have an  opportunity to redeem yourself when the  game enters reflex mode this bullet time  like slow-motion effect gives you a few  seconds to disable your spotter with  short-range attacks or a well-placed  shot to the head in order to avoid  triggering a base-wide alert no matter  how careful you are it's a given that  you'll eventually be spotted immediately  alarms blare and enemies sometimes and  armored vehicles flock to your last  known location in the past I handy icon  in a countdown timer would help you  determine where your enemies are and how  long you have to remain in hiding before  they give up their search now with those  features removed you have to monitor  live radio communications in order to  gather the same Intel nothing is simply  handed to you in ground zeroes is a far  more tense and rewarding experience for  it how well you manage to complete your  objectives determines the rain  you earn at the end of a mission and  everything from the number of times  you're spotted to the amount of  casualties you caused along the way has  an effect on your score and the rewards  you earn such rewards include weapon  drops at the start of missions and in  mission trials that can significantly  alter your trajectory during their peak  playthroughs rankings aside you don't  need to sneak in the shadows if you  don't want to and with an entire  military base worth of toys at your  disposal sometimes it's fun to stand  tall and announce your presence armed  with c4 and RPGs you're fully prepared  to blow up vehicles destroy watchtowers  and charge in guns blazing when the mood  strikes you the dichotomy that exists  between stealth and action-oriented  gameplay lends itself to fear tension  and excitement one moment you can hear a  pin drop and the next you're bolting  across a chaotic military base with  bullets whizzing by your head and  desperation clouding your focus if this  was a more linear experience perhaps the  allure of this contrast might wear thin  but given the options at your disposal  ground zeroes almost never gets old  unfortunately if you hope to be  entertained by an equal helping of metal  gears typically perplexing and  intriguing narrative you're almost  certainly going to be disappointed  there's some fat to savor here but once  you've digested the cutscenes that  bookend the main mission it's a pure  gameplay driven experience from then on  out though it's unusual for everything  to take place in a single location  there's so much to do and see and  whether you take a stealthy or head-on  approach infiltrating camp Omega is a  thrilling experience that shouldn't be  missed  you  [Music]

